// 30_stream_consume.mty — v0.29 Track D: source-level `while let`.
//
// v0.27 Track E shipped the streaming runtime (`MessageStream::next`
// + `next_blocking`, plus the `Stream` trait impl) and v0.27's
// example 29 set up the `_drain_stream` helper. What was missing was
// the surface-syntax loop: a Mighty `.mty` program had to use a
// bounded `for _i in 0..k` shape because the parser couldn't accept
// `while let Some(delta) = stream.next() { ... }`.
//
// Track D lands the `while let` parser + HIR lowering, so the
// canonical streaming loop now compiles as written. This example
// pins the SHAPE down — `mty check` must keep accepting it, and the
// surrounding stream stub keeps the demo mounted in the LLM
// streaming story.
//
// Build:
//   mty fmt examples/30_stream_consume.mty
//   mty check examples/30_stream_consume.mty
//
// We don't `mty run` against a live model — the `MessageStream` is
// opaque to the SIR interpreter and `next()` returns `None` for
// non-array receivers (which makes the loop exit immediately). The
// canonical Rust-side test lives in
// `crates/mty-stdlib/tests/llm_streaming_source.rs`, and an interp-
// level test for the `while let` lowering itself lives in
// `crates/mty-driver/tests/interp_runnable.rs`
// (`while_let_*` tests).
// ----- Source-level streaming surface -----------------------------------
//
// `_drain_stream` pulls deltas off the typed handle until the
// upstream stream is exhausted. The Mighty source matches the
// snippet baked into the `MessageStream::next` doc comment
// one-for-one:
//
//   while let Some(delta) = stream.next() { handle(delta) }
//
// On the SIR interpreter (`mty check` / fixture tests) the opaque
// receiver returns `None` from the very first `.next()` so the loop
// runs zero iterations. On the AOT runtime the loop drives the real
// async stream through `next_blocking`.
fn _drain_stream(stream: MessageStream) {
  while let Some(_delta) = stream.next() {
    log("delta")
  }
}

// `_consume_with_break` shows that `break` works the same inside
// `while let` as inside plain `while` — the loop frame the lowering
// installs routes break back to the same exit target, so a
// streaming consumer can short-circuit on the first sentinel delta
// without losing the pattern-match semantics.
fn _consume_with_break(stream: MessageStream) {
  while let Some(_delta) = stream.next() {
    log("delta")
    break
  }
}

// ----- Provider stub ----------------------------------------------------
//
// `make_stream` stands in for a real `client.complete_stream(req)`
// call; returning the opaque handle without invoking a live model
// keeps `mty check` deterministic. The real call site lives in the
// Rust-side streaming test.
fn make_stream() -> MessageStream {
  MessageStream.empty()
}

// ----- Entry point ------------------------------------------------------
//
// `main` exercises only the parser + checker surface — same
// discipline as example 29. The helpers above are the regression
// hooks; `main` keeps the example mounted in the workspace sweep.
fn main() {
  let s = make_stream()
  _drain_stream(s)
  log("30_stream_consume: while let Some(delta) = stream.next() { ... }")
}
